<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narshakhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">899–959</span> · Also known as Abu Bakr Narshakhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narshakhi (Abu Bakr Muhammad Narshakhi) was a historian from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> oasis who in 943 or 944 wrote a History of Bukhara in Arabic for the Samanid ruler Nuh I. The work survives only in a shortened Persian translation of 1128 with later additions. It is the main source for the pre-Islamic and early Islamic history of Bukhara, including its local rulers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudahs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narshakhi.jpg" alt="Narshakhi" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernest Leroux, 1892 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DeBoukhara_Cover.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
